  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Relate an LTI system's impulse response $h(t)$ to its transfer function $H(s)$ by using the Laplace transform to connect the time-domain convolution kernel with the $s$-domain multiplier.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "An LTI system is described in the time domain by its impulse response $h(t)$. The corresponding $s$-domain description is the transfer function $H(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{h(t)\\}$. In the simplest base case, $h(t)=\\delta(t)$, so the system has $H(s)=1$ and does not change the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=\\delta(t) \\Rightarrow H(s)=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For any input $x(t)$, the output of an LTI system is $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$. Taking the one-sided Laplace transform gives $Y(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{y(t)\\}=X(s)H(s)$. This is the rule that connects the time-domain convolution kernel to the $s$-domain multiplier.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)*h(t)\\}=X(s)H(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $h(t)$ when you want the intuitive time-domain description of the system, and use $H(s)$ when you want the algebraic multiplier that acts on $X(s)$. The two forms describe the same LTI system, so later sections can move between them without changing the system itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The practical takeaway is simple: recognize the impulse response, transform it to get the transfer function, and then use $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$ to analyze the output in the Laplace domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying $h(t)$ as the Time-Domain System Description",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From $y(t)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(\\tau)e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$, identify the corresponding $h(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the given output formula to the convolution pattern $y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau$. The cue is the factor multiplying $x(\\tau)$ inside the integral, so the kernel is the part that depends on $t-\\tau$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here, $e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)$ plays the role of $h(t-\\tau)$. To read off the time-domain system description, replace the shifted argument $t-\\tau$ by the free variable $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "h(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the impulse response is $h(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$. That is the time-domain description of the LTI system, and it is the quantity whose Laplace transform later gives the transfer function $H(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding $H(s)$ from a Simple Impulse Response",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $H(s)$ for $h(t)=2e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a causal exponential impulse response. Use the standard one-sided Laplace pair for $e^{-at}u(t)$, then keep the constant multiplier outside the transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+a}, \\qquad \\Re(s)>-a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "With $a=2$, we get $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-2t}u(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s+2}$. Multiplying by the outside factor 2 gives $H(s)=\\frac{2}{s+2}$, with ROC $\\Re(s)>-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding $h(t)$ from a Simple Transfer Function",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $h(t)$ when $H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a simple first-order transfer function with one pole at $s=-2$. Match it to the standard unilateral Laplace pair, then read the time-domain kernel by reversing the transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\frac{1}{s+a}\\right\\}=e^{-at}u(t), \\qquad \\Re(s)>-a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the denominator is $s+2$, so set $a=2$. The inverse transform is $h(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$, with causal ROC $\\Re(s)>-2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Transfer Function vs. Impulse Response",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing $y(t)=x(t)*h(t) with Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{-t}u(t)$ and $h(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$, show $y(t)=x(t)*h(t)$ and verify $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that both signals are causal exponentials, so each one has a standard one-sided Laplace transform. First compute the input transform and the impulse-response transform, then multiply them in the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}, \\qquad H(s)=\\frac{1}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the system rule $Y(s)=X(s)H(s)$. The product is $Y(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}$, which is the $s$-domain form that matches the convolution output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "Y(s)=X(s)H(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To check consistency in time, convolve the signals directly: $y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-\\tau}e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau=(e^{-t}-e^{-2t})u(t)$. That time-domain result transforms back to the same product, so the convolution rule and the multiplication rule agree.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
